{"author":{"id":"1d5f412cdeafe9987ad9876b0995bf5032718543d34c038a50cdb41808e9874c"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788305137,"nonce":"4BohiA4OpCD7alDH9Cf9PiMY+84=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOULVc1M8AAAABPPhkjQ","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/torram-ng/issues/19","origin":"github"},"title":"bencode.py returns str or bytes depending on the data, breaking non-UTF-8 paths","message":"The `bencode.py` dependency decodes bencoded byte strings opportunistically — it attempts UTF-8 and falls back to `bytes`. Verified against 4.1.0:\n\n```\nname    -\u003e str    \"hello\"\npieces  -\u003e bytes  b\"\\x80\\x81\\x82...\"\n```\n\nSo the Python type of a field depends on whether its bytes happen to be valid UTF-8.\n\nThe practical failure is torrents with non-UTF-8 filenames (Shift-JIS, cp1251 and similar are common in older and regional torrents). Their `path` lists come back as a mix of `str` and `bytes`, and `os.path.join(*file_info[\"path\"])` raises:\n\n```\nTypeError: Can't mix strings and bytes in path components\n```\n\nThe correct behaviour is to keep everything as `bytes` at the parser boundary and decode explicitly, honouring the `encoding` key when present and falling back to UTF-8 with `surrogateescape` so the value round-trips through the filesystem APIs.\n\nThis is also a correctness hazard for `pieces`, which must never be decoded.","files":null}]}